      'A simple sentence has one independent clause: Mina checked the map. A compound sentence coordinates independent clauses: Mina checked the map, and Arun packed water. A complex sentence adds a dependent clause: Before they left, Mina checked the map. Vary structure to show relationships, not just to make a sentence longer. A short sentence can emphasize a turning point: The bridge was closed. A longer sentence can explain circumstances. Two independent clauses need an appropriate boundary; a comma alone makes a comma splice in standard edited English.',
