      'Argument Writing',
      [
        'Develop a qualified and contestable claim.',
        'Support it with evidence and an explicit warrant.',
        'Answer a counterargument without misrepresentation.'
      ],
      'Argument writing defends a position through reasoning. A workable claim is Our school should trial a bottle-refill station near the courtyard. Evidence might include a documented queue at the existing station; the warrant explains that a second accessible point could reduce waiting. This is a plausible connection, not a guaranteed result. A counterargument might identify maintenance cost. Respond with a limited trial and planned cost review rather than pretending cost does not exist. The model uses invented observations for learning; real writing needs real records, clear attribution and appropriate permissions for any data collection.',
      'Draft the core argument. 1. State a limited trial proposal. 2. Present a relevant observed queue. 3. Explain how access may affect waiting. 4. Acknowledge maintenance. 5. Propose monitoring both use and cost.',
      'Write a response to The new station may create maintenance work. Use a concession and a feasible response.',
      'Maintenance is a real concern, so the trial should record cleaning time and repair costs before any permanent decision. This concedes the issue and proposes evidence rather than dismissal.',
