      ],
      'Style includes diction, syntax and rhythm. Compare these original sentences: After checking the map twice and testing the loose board, Mina crossed the bridge. Mina crossed. The longer sentence foregrounds preparation; the short one creates abrupt emphasis but omits those details. A periodic sentence delays its main clause, building anticipation. A cumulative sentence gives the main clause first and adds detail afterward. Concision removes needless repetition, not useful qualification. Because the sample was small, the result is uncertain should not become The result is certain merely to be shorter. Style choices must serve audience, purpose and meaning.',
      'Revise Due to the fact that the path was flooded, we made a decision to return. 1. Replace due to the fact that with because. 2. Replace made a decision to return with decided to return. 3. Keep the reason and action: Because the path flooded, we decided to return.',
