      'Research begins with a question narrow enough to investigate: How did library use change during a four-week evening-opening trial at one school? This is more answerable than Are libraries good? Choose evidence that addresses the question: attendance records, opening dates and a clear description of the recording method. Keep a source log with author or organization, title, date, location and relevant page or section. Separate exact quotations, paraphrases and your own analysis in notes. The model trial is invented; real research requires authentic records and permission for any private data. A search result snippet is a lead, not a substitute for reading the source.',
