      ],
      'Critical reading asks what a text claims and how it supports that claim. Invented report: After the school added evening opening, visits increased. Therefore evening opening alone caused the rise. The first sentence reports a sequence; the conclusion adds a causal claim. New class projects, changed schedules or different counting may also explain part of the rise. A heading such as A Perfect Success frames the event positively but supplies no additional evidence. Check source methods, definitions, omissions and plausible alternatives. Critical reading means fair evaluation, not automatically rejecting every source or assuming a hidden motive.',
      'Assess the report. 1. Separate observed increase from causal inference. 2. Identify alone as a strong claim. 3. Ask about other changes and comparable records. 4. State that the timing is consistent with an effect but does not isolate the cause.',
      'An advertisement says Nine users praised the tool, so it works for everyone. What assumptions and missing details should a reader notice?',
      'The claim assumes nine selected users represent everyone. It omits selection method, negative responses and the definition of works. Praise alone does not establish universal effectiveness.',
