      'A research report should connect a question, methods, findings and interpretation. In an invented library trial, the question is whether recorded use changed during four extra evening sessions. Methods explain the dates, counting process and comparison period. Findings state recorded visits; discussion considers what the pattern means and its limits. Increased visits do not by themselves prove improved grades. Attribute borrowed ideas and quote accurately, using the assigned citation style. The invented trial model teaches organization only; a real report must use authentic data and protect private participant information.',
